* Appending to a slice

It is common to append new elements to a slice, and so Go provides a built-in
⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000001⟫append⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000002⟫ function. The [[⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000003⟫][documentation]]
of the built-in package describes ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000004⟫append⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000005⟫.

⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000006⟫

The first parameter ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000007⟫s⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000008⟫ of ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000009⟫append⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000010⟫ is a slice of type ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000011⟫T⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000012⟫, and the rest are
⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000013⟫T⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000014⟫ values to append to the slice.

The resulting value of ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000015⟫append⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000016⟫ is a slice containing all the elements of the
original slice plus the provided values.

If the backing array of ⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000017⟫s⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000018⟫ is too small to fit all the given values a bigger
array will be allocated. The returned slice will point to the newly allocated
array.

(To learn more about slices, read the [[⟪GTI18N_f2fc257e_000019⟫][Slices: usage and internals]] article.)

